# Development Rules

- Never add comments to code or configuration files.
- Preserve all existing comments unless the user explicitly requests their removal.
- Never remove an existing comment containing `todo`, regardless of capitalization, unless the user explicitly requests it.
- Write a JavaScript named import with exactly one imported name on one line: `import { name } from './module.js'`.
- When a JavaScript named import has two or more imported names, place every imported name on its own line inside a multiline import block.
- Keep an HTML or JSX opening tag with exactly one attribute on one line.
- When an HTML or JSX opening tag has two or more attributes, use a multiline opening tag and place every attribute on its own line.
- Never wrap returned JSX in parentheses.
- Use camelCase for every identifier and every platform-owned filename across development and production, including variables, functions, parameters, constants, classes, enums, properties, directories, and generated names.
- Use a non-camelCase name only when camelCase would prevent the code or system from running, building, deploying, or interoperating correctly.
- When non-camelCase is technically required, use only the exact alternative format required by the external constraint, such as lowercase Azure Storage container names.
- In JavaScript, return `clientError` or `serverError` whenever applicable instead of introducing another error-response pattern.
- Never use double quotes in `.js` or `.jsx` files.
- Use single quotes for strings in `.js` and `.jsx` files. When a string contains a single quote, use backticks instead of escaping the single quote or using double quotes.
- Remove all trailing whitespace from every line in every file, including single spaces, multiple spaces, and tabs.
- When a `.js` or `.jsx` file exports only one default value, export it anonymously unless that value is reused within the same file.
- When a `.js` or `.jsx` file has exactly one named export, the exported name must exactly match the filename without its extension.
- Never use the `glob` package or API. Use `fast-glob` for all globbing.
- Never use GUID terminology or implementations. Use UUID for every globally unique identifier across the platform.
- When creating a YAML file with an extension, use `.yaml` and never `.yml`.
- In `.js` and `.jsx` files, always use arrow functions and never use function declaration statements.
- In `.js` and `.jsx` files, omit parentheses around an arrow function's single identifier parameter.
- In `.js` and `.jsx` files, keep an object destructuring expression with exactly one property on one line.
- When an object destructuring expression in a `.js` or `.jsx` file has two or more properties, place every destructured property on its own line, including destructuring in variables, constants, assignments, and parameters.
- Always format a ternary expression across exactly five parts on separate lines: the condition, `?`, the true expression, `:`, and the false expression.
